@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 7. 安全模型：**如果要暴露到局域网**：`allowRemote: true` 只是关掉 loopback 白名单，**不会**增加任何认证强度。那种部署应放在 TLS 终止代理后面并另配访问控制。

**共享机器**：token 文件就是凭据。不要抄进 shell 历史、CI 明文变量或聊天记录；怀疑泄漏就删掉该文件并重载插件——会重新生成，旧 token 立即失效。

---

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 8. 端口与连接稳定性：DSH 的 Web 服务端口由启动器决定，桌面端交给系统分配，所以**重启后会变**。本插件不固定端口。

**为什么不在 patch 里写死**：`dsh-web-app` 的 webserver 行用 `!!js` 取 `ctx.webStartup.port ?? 3080`；而 `cordis.patch.yml` 的覆写是**整行 config 替换**，写死就要把 `host` / `tls` / `compression` 等键全部重述，官方升级引入新键时会静默丢掉。端口被占时 DSH 还会起不来。

**端点由插件发布**：每次激活调用 `ctx.webServer.getOrigin()` 拿真实地址写进 `endpoint.json`；客户端（CLI / MCP / `lib/client.mjs`）**每次调用都重新读**，不缓存。

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 8. 端口与连接稳定性：**调用方断开之后**：非流式请求在 socket 关闭时会中止这次调用——事件迭代停下、运行槽位释放，但**那一轮不会取消**，它仍在 Host 里继续跑，之后还能用 `wait` 收（GUI 里也看得到）。这是 abort 与 cancel 的区别。

**被中止的调用怎么记账**：记在 `/status` 的 `calls.outcomes`，不是 `errors`——`timeouts` 是"等到预算用完"，`cancelledByClient` 是"调用方先走了"。

---

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 11. 验证：`npm test` 按固定顺序跑这十段；`npm run test:host` 跑真宿主集成；`npm run test:e2e` / `npm run test:edge` 要活的 Harness。

**别用 `node --test`**：它会为每个文件 fork 子进程，在 DSH 沙箱里被 `spawn EPERM` 挡住。

几个值得单独说的：

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · What it is：The port is **not fixed**: DSH gets one from the OS at each start, so it changes after a restart. `node bin/dsh-rpc.mjs
ping` prints the current `origin`; the plugin also writes it to `endpoint.json`, which clients re-read on every call
(see §8).

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 1.1 MCP (recommended)：`bin/` means **the package's** `bin/` directory. Installed from npm (the recommended route, §6) it lives under
`%USERPROFILE%\.dsh\profiles\desktop\node_modules\@punchact\dsh-rpc-agent\`.

```powershell
claude mcp add dsh -- node "$env:USERPROFILE\.dsh\profiles\desktop\node_modules\@punchact\dsh-rpc-agent\bin\dsh-rpc-mcp.mjs"
```

Cursor / Codex use the equivalent config-file form (remember JSON escaping on Windows):

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "dsh": {
      "command": "node",

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 1.1 MCP (recommended)：Installing from a local clone instead? Swap the path for `<path-to-your-clone>\bin\dsh-rpc-mcp.mjs`. Everywhere
else this document writes `node bin/...`, meaning "run it with the current directory inside the package".

It exposes **9 tools**:

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 1.1.1 Sticky sessions：Two boundaries: **clients never inherit each other's sessions** (`clientInfo.name` is part of the namespace), and a
recorded session that was deleted or is still blank means "create a new one", not an error. The table is written
atomically with mode 0600; a corrupt file simply means "no record".

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 1.1.1 Sticky sessions：One session eventually becomes a context burden, so after **200** `ask` deliveries on the same key the plugin
rotates: it creates a new session and reports the old id as `previousSessionId` (`rotated: true`). That threshold
counts **this bridge's deliveries**, not Host messages — the Host has no cheap counter.

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 1.3 Plain HTTP：```powershell
$endpoint = (Get-Content "$env:USERPROFILE\.dsh\dsh-rpc\endpoint.json" -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json).url
$token    = (Get-Content "$env:USERPROFILE\.dsh\dsh-rpc\token" -Raw).Trim()
$body = @{ jsonrpc = "2.0"; id = 1; method = "dsh.sessions.ask";
           params = @{ text = "reply with one word: ok"; cwd = "C:\path\to\work" } } | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 6

`endpoint.json` is rewritten at every start, so reading the address this way needs no knowledge of the port.

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 1.4 JS client：```js
import { rpcCall } from "@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent/http-client";

const answer = await rpcCall("dsh.sessions.ask", { text: "summarize this repository", cwd: process.cwd() });
console.log(answer.text, answer.usage);

The subpath is declared in `exports` (`"./http-client": "./lib/client.mjs"`); in a local clone it is
`import … from "<path-to-your-clone>/lib/client.mjs"`.

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 1.4 JS client：`rpcCall` resolves to the method's `result`; on failure it throws an Error carrying `code` / `data` / `status`, so
you can branch on the code.

---

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 3.4 Workspaces, models, effort, permissions：**Model names** may be `provider/model`; a bare id is looked up in the catalogue — one match is used, several
produce an error listing the unambiguous spellings, none tells you to look at `dsh.models`. **It never guesses.**
`reasoningEffort` must accompany `model` in the same request: the Host has no "read back the current model" call.

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 3.4 Workspaces, models, effort, permissions：1. The turn result carries `sandbox.denied: true` and `next`;
2. `next.args` carries only `session_id`: **deciding to re-ask with `danger-full-access` is yours to make**. The
   bridge neither decides for you nor stuffs that argument into the result (otherwise a blocked agent could talk its
   way into an escalation);
3. **Escalate once.** That preset's approval policy is `never`, so nothing blocks that turn afterwards — it is not a
   "wider default", it is the last resort when blocked, and you should say so in your answer.

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 3.5 Streaming endpoint：`POST /dsh-rpc/stream` takes the same body as `ask` and answers one JSON per line:

```jsonc
{ "type": "session", "sessionId": "session-…", "cwd": "…", "agentPreset": null }   // only when created
{ "type": "progress", "frame": { "type": "event", "event": { "type": "turn/start" } } }
{ "type": "result", "sessionId": "session-…", "status": "completed" }              // last frame
{ "type": "error", "error": { "kind": "…", "message": "…" } }                      // or this frame

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 6. Install and update：`bin/` and `test/` are separate processes and need no reload. The release flow is documented in
[docs/release.md](./docs/release.md).

---

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 8. Ports and connection stability：```powershell
node bin/dsh-rpc.mjs health --wait 15000; if ($?) { node bin/dsh-rpc.mjs ask "keep going" --session session-xxxx }
```

`--wait` re-reads the endpoint every 250 ms and probes until ready (exit 0 with the elapsed time), timing out with
exit 3.

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 8. Ports and connection stability：**Cases that still fail**: the plugin not yet active while the Harness is starting (the `endpoint.json` still holds
the previous address), and multiple instances running at once (single file, last writer wins). Use `--url`
explicitly for both.

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 8. Ports and connection stability：**After the caller disconnects**: a non-streaming request aborts when the socket closes — event iteration stops and
the run slot is released, but **the turn is not cancelled**. It keeps running in the Host and can still be collected
with `wait` (and is visible in the GUI). That is the difference between abort and cancel.

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 8. Ports and connection stability：**How aborted calls are accounted**: under `calls.outcomes` in `/status`, not `errors` — `timeouts` means "the
budget ran out", `cancelledByClient` means "the caller left first".

---

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 9. Following a CLI task in the GUI：This works because the plugin goes through the Host's `sessionController` rather than taking a bare agent: session
log, title generation and `running` state all come from the same store.

Three practical points:

@punchact/dsh-rpc-agent README · 10. Status panel in Settings：It reads **`GET /dsh-rpc/status`**, which shares the `/health` trust boundary — **loopback only and no token**,
because a page in the browser cannot read the token in `endpoint.json`. In exchange that endpoint must say nothing:

- no token, no `sessionId` (active runs expose only the first 8 characters and whether a session exists);
- no call arguments at all — no prompt text, no tool parameters. Call accounting stores only **the method name, the
  duration and the result code**.
